{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 9,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.137,
            0.046,
            0.347,
            0.064
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 8"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.087,
            0.847,
            0.125
          ],
          "content": "linked proportionally, out of which three are known and we must find the fourth, unknown, quantity."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.125,
            0.848,
            0.163
          ],
          "content": "To solve such problems, we can model two proportional ratios using algebraic notation as"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.442,
            0.162,
            0.556,
            0.181
          ],
          "content": "a : b : : c : d .",
          "image_source": "images/page_9_equation_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.186,
            0.848,
            0.243
          ],
          "content": "For these two ratios to be proportional, we know that term c should be a multiple of term a by a factor, say $f ,$ and term d should be a multiple of term b by the same factor f. So,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.43,
            0.246,
            0.568,
            0.291
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{aligned}c &= f a \\quad \\ldots (1) \\\\d &= f b \\quad \\ldots (2)\\end{aligned}",
          "image_source": "images/page_9_equation_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.174,
            0.294,
            0.503,
            0.315
          ],
          "content": "From (1) and (2), we can say that,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.414,
            0.316,
            0.58,
            0.351
          ],
          "content": "f = \\frac{c}{a}  and  f = \\frac{d}{b}.",
          "image_source": "images/page_9_equation_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.413,
            0.353,
            0.583,
            0.386
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{Therefore}, \\frac{c}{a} = \\frac{d}{b}.",
          "image_source": "images/page_9_equation_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.174,
            0.39,
            0.533,
            0.411
          ],
          "content": "Multiplying both sides by ab, we get,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.433,
            0.414,
            0.593,
            0.447
          ],
          "content": "ab \\times \\frac{c}{a} = ab \\times \\frac{d}{b}",
          "image_source": "images/page_9_equation_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.431,
            0.449,
            0.592,
            0.484
          ],
          "content": "ab \\times \\frac{c}{a} = ab \\times \\frac{d}{b}",
          "image_source": "images/page_9_equation_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.423,
            0.486,
            0.6,
            0.506
          ],
          "content": "b\\bar{c} = ad\\  or\\ ad = bc",
          "image_source": "images/page_9_equation_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.508,
            0.848,
            0.545
          ],
          "content": "Thus, when a : b :: c : d, then ad = bc. This is known as cross multiplication of terms."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.174,
            0.545,
            0.486,
            0.565
          ],
          "content": "Since ad $= b c ,$ we can show that"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.46,
            0.568,
            0.536,
            0.599
          ],
          "content": "d = { \\frac { b c } { a } } .",
          "image_source": "images/page_9_equation_15.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.162,
            0.616,
            0.835,
            0.675
          ],
          "content": "Two ratios are proportional if their terms are equal when cross multiplied. The fourth unknown quantity can be found through such cross multiplication."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.686,
            0.848,
            0.78
          ],
          "content": "In ancient India, Āryabhaṭa (199 CE) and others called such problems of proportionality Rule of Three problems. There were 3 numbers given—the pramāṇa (measure $- \\mathbf { a } ^ { \\prime }$ in our case), the phala (fruit — ‘b’ in our case), and the ichchhā (requisition $- c$ in our case). To find the ichchhāphala (yield—‘d’ in our case), Āryabhaṭa says,"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.779,
            0.848,
            0.816
          ],
          "content": "“Multiply the phala by the ichchhā and divide the resulting product by the pramāṇa.”"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.816,
            0.489,
            0.835
          ],
          "content": "In other words, Āryabhaṭa says,"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.247,
            0.834,
            0.775,
            0.854
          ],
          "content": "“pramāṇa : phala :: ichchhā : ichchhāphala,” therefore,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.299,
            0.858,
            0.721,
            0.88
          ],
          "content": "p r a m { \\bar { a } } n a \\times i c h c h h { \\bar { a } } p h a l a = p h a l a \\times i c h c h h { \\bar { a } } .",
          "image_source": "images/page_9_equation_21.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.078,
            0.914,
            0.128,
            0.938
          ],
          "content": ""
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.448,
